{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The lexicographic product G ∘ H of two graphs, both directed or both undirected, as a lazy"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"view. Made by "},{"code":"lexicographicProduct(_:_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". Also called the composition G[H]."}],"kind":"symbol","metadata":{"role":"symbol","externalID":"s:13GraphProducts20LexicographicProductV","modules":[{"name":"GraphProducts"}],"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"LexicographicProduct","kind":"identifier"}],"roleHeading":"Structure","symbolKind":"struct","title":"LexicographicProduct","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"LexicographicProduct"}],"remoteSource":{"fileName":"LexicographicProduct.swift","url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/GraphProducts\/LexicographicProduct.swift#L39"}},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"kind":"attribute","text":"@frozen"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"LexicographicProduct"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"genericParameter","text":"First"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"kind":"genericParameter","text":"Second"},{"kind":"text","text":">"}]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"overview","text":"Overview","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Vertices and their indices are as in ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"CartesianProduct"},{"text":". The row of (g, h) lists ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"(g′, h′)"},{"text":" for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"each entry g′ of G’s row and ","type":"text"},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"every","type":"text"}]},{"text":" h′ of H, in index order, then the H-moves ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"(g, h′)"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"An edge’s position is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".first(edge: e, uVertexIndex: s, vVertexIndex: t)"},{"type":"text","text":", G’s edge "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"e"},{"type":"text","text":" with"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"H-vertex index "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"s"},{"type":"text","text":" at its "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"u"},{"type":"text","text":" end (directed: its source) and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"t"},{"type":"text","text":" at its "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"type":"text","text":" end (its target), or"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".second(vertexIndex: g, edge: f)"},{"type":"text","text":", H’s edge "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"f"},{"type":"text","text":" at G-vertex index "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"g"},{"type":"text","text":". Over an undirected"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"self-loop of G only "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"s ≤ t"},{"type":"text","text":" occurs, since (a, s)–(a, t) and (a, t)–(a, s) are one edge; a row"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"lists such a loop’s edges at its first appearance, and only the product loop again at its"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"second. Directed, the product has edge indices when both factors do:"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"index(e)·|V(H)|² + s·|V(H)| + t"},{"type":"text","text":", then "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"m_G·|V(H)|² + g·m_H + index(f)"},{"type":"text","text":". Undirected it has none."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"sections":[],"topicSections":[{"anchor":"Structures","generated":true,"title":"Structures","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/LexicographicProduct\/DirectedEdges","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/LexicographicProduct\/EdgeRow","doc:\/\/Gr